<title>FRANCE: French crude imports rose in May.</title>
<headline>French crude imports rose in May.</headline>
<dateline>PARIS 1997-07-17</dateline>
<text>
<p>France's industry ministry issued</p>
<p>the following data for deliveries to refineries, margins and</p>
<p>capacity utilisation. Crude deliveries by origin ('000 tonnes).</p>
<p>					    May 1997   Apr 1997 May 1996</p>
<p>    Total				 7,886	7,422    6,748</p>
<p>    Middle East			 2,768	2,700    2,576</p>
<p>    including Saudi Arabia	  1,276	1,490    1,673</p>
<p>		  Iraq			643	  330	  0</p>
<p>		  Iran			434	  628	614</p>
<p>		  Kuwait			0	   13	  0</p>
<p>    Africa				  555	1,295    1,476</p>
<p>    including Algeria		   231	  353	239</p>
<p>		  Libya		     196	  319	106</p>
<p>		  Nigeria		   128	    0	999</p>
<p>    OECD Europe			 3,611	2,736    1,732</p>
<p>    including Britain		 1,359	1,305	695</p>
<p>		  Norway		  2,051	1,198	836</p>
<p>		  France		    146	  151	201</p>
<p>    Other				   953	  690	964</p>
<p>    including Venezuela		   0	    0</p>
<p>		  Mexico			0	    0</p>
<p>		  ex-USSR		   953	  690	964</p>
<p>The ministry also gave the following data for French</p>
<p>refining margins, based on North Sea Brent crude ($/barrel).</p>
<p>			    Jun 1997  May 1997  Apr 1997  Jun 1996</p>
<p>    Refining Margin     2.95	2.26	3.31	1.62</p>
<p>In the most recent month for which refinery capacity utilisation is available, French plants ran at 92 percent of capacity in May, up from from 88 percent in April but down from 94 percent in May 1996.</p>
<p>-- Paris newsroom +331 4221 5146</p>
</text>
